vectorwake (criccomini, push to main): Subspace-inspired space MMO, design phase. Defold client, deterministic C99 sim core, authoritative Rust server, sandboxed modules; own art/ships; AI bots emit inputs only; damage-weighted Elo. Docs in docs/.                                        
vectorwake built: duel mode + decisions closed (100Hz, nose aim), sim core with flight and combat verified deterministic on 3 ABIs, playable wasm web client with AI pilots; criccomini: macOS+web, autonomous, push to main.                                                                  
vectorwake server complete through duels: 100Hz authoritative arenas, prizes, inelastic walls, damage-weighted Elo, AI pilots, multi-arena zone with modes; controls are arrows plus Space/Shift.                                                                                              
M5 zone surface, duel/warzone modes, and the Defold client all landed; Defold gotchas: JDK25, extern C, graphics constants, release strips draw_debug3d, sim.init before connect.                                                                                                              
vectorwake reached a shippable client: mesh vector rendering, prototype-matched HUD, sound, bot fixes, published to artifact 8a789853.                                                                                                                                                         
Multiplayer works end to end (join a zone, wss, generated call signs) and maps became typed tiles loaded from files.                                                                                                                                                                           
Web client work: check build exit codes (a pipe hid a build error for 3 publishes), unlock audio on any gesture, profile the browser not native, vwbuf cut Lua frame cost 3x. You land flying; escape opens a menu tree. Weapons are one table model. Bombs never splash on timeout.           
Zones own their rules: settings ship over the wire and weapons are named in the zone file. The tech tree is levels, add-ons, charges and untyped greens. Bounty is derived from what you hold; points are the victim's bounty, so a fresh spawn is worth nothing.                              
Tuning comes from ASSS dist/conf/svs, not invention: the original gates almost nothing per ship, rounds are slower than ships deliberately, multifire costs +50% energy +100% cooldown. spawn_prizes=30 flattens bot skill so calibration pins it 0. Arena is the full 1024-tile map.          
A pass over cost and interface: Rust mirror sizes now asserted, vec overflow culled, snapshots halved by tile coords plus an interest radius, sim_step a third faster, and the mobile HUD stripped to a d-pad, three right-hand pads and an info toggle.                                       
Touch flight: the stick asks for a course, not a facing, and a thumb arcs, so sweeps hold the rudder until it lands. Zone model redesigned (decisions 23-26): one arena per process, directories only report, arenas pick their own zone. Duels removed for now, decision 16 deferred.         
Hosting on Vultr; a room is 79 KB and 16 us/tick at 64 ships, so cost is egress not compute. ASSS ships no per-ship balance and never capped an arena, so our roster and our 64-ship limit are both ours. Repel is hostile only now, firer included.                                           
Fleet design is settled in docs (no chat, DNS directories, dynamic rooms), the roster now runs the original's exact numbers with every setting exposed in zone.toml, and bots finally bomb. Mirror bugs are caught by asserting offsets, not sizeof.                                           
The game got its balance measured (hulls flat, tech tree is the difference, bots never bomb) and then got deployed: live on one Vultr box behind Caddy, hardened end to end after the blind-deploy lessons.                                                                                    
Real clients and real play, not health checks, are what found the bugs: the recharge overflow, a refusal loop, then three stacked reasons Chaos was dead. Bots also learned to perceive like players, and the deploy path became a git pull.                                                   
A long day of things that looked healthy and were not: unreachable flags, unanswered pings, a dead kill branch, a browser no phone could tap, plus repel matched to nullspace and the build moved to CI.                                                                                       
Growing and repairing the live fleet: a stale socket, a lost certificate store, a ladder that never shipped and a build context missing a compiled-in file, plus Alpha joining the browse list on an arena of its own.                                                                         
2026-08-03 Fixed: arrivals got no spawn kit, bots pinned on unreachable greens, a stale ladder, and input timing. Client offline mode removed so the menu is home and pause. Decision 29 designed: bots leave the arena tick for a server of declared clients.                                 
Bots fly live from a bot server subcommand (51 in chaos and war, 19 in alpha); hulls and map art both drawn in the vector language, with the lvl format documented and converted. Sound is synthesised at boot by sfx.c, halving the page to 1.55 MB gzipped.                                  
One day's art and infrastructure: interface redrawn without boxes, maps closed by sim_map_index, vwbuf memsets what buffer.create leaves dirty, strokes feathered a pixel against flicker, one-shot audio bypasses the mixer queue, hulls bank on turns. All measured before shipping.         
Client work to 2026-08-04: turning asteroids, tile readout, terrain-only map view on M, catalog v7 zone settings, and four quiet failures (nil globals now linted, sub-pixel blip blink, a Lua error in a draw freezing the frame, sim_unpack's memset re-firing held inputs).                 
Collision is per-hull now, the home screen is the 'wake' design, and the radar dial is anchored to the map's grid. Bots stopped grinding walls with capsule whiskers. Audio has a design doc, and client-defold.md was rewritten from a stale plan to the client that exists.                  
Bots: the merged brains (crawl 8-13%, 0.76 kills/bot-min) and a shared Rig per arena cutting the bot server 1.4 cores to 0.4. Client: the authoritative S2C_KILL feed, and iOS Safari's landscape stretch took a vendored template, a 4Hz rect-verified reconciler and no-cache.               
The iPhone confirmed the rect-verify pin; the client batch shipped (explosions, rung colors, field doors, tumbling rocks, favicon), prod stopped serving --fragment; teams got a design (decision 32) and alpha runs 10 numeric teams since the 64 seats are the population.                   
Client: no hit boxes in the field of play (left click is the gun); the games list redials its directory with a generation guard; team names dodge the bot roster and are named to others only when public; a repel lives one tick, so remote ones draw off the charge count.                   
